title: Watch sources and regimes
tags: [[Watcher]]
kind: reference
summary: The budget regime places one inotify watch per eligible directory; the coverage regime (fanotify) covers whole filesystems — the rest of the pipeline is the same.

Two regimes, by how a source covers a tree:

* ''Budget'' — the `notify` source: inotify on Linux, one registration per directory. A watch set is computed, placed and maintained; the supply is finite ([[The watch budget]]). It runs on every platform (on macOS and Windows too, placing one registration per directory, the budget being inert where no limit is readable).
* ''Coverage'' — the fanotify source, through a privileged broker ([[The fanotify broker]]): one ''filesystem'' mark per filesystem the tree spans — the root's and each mounted beneath it, followed as drives come and go. Nothing to place, nothing to run out of.

One source is active per repository — fanotify when `[settings] watchd-socket` answers
at load, the `notify` backend otherwise, never both (every change would be recorded
twice). A socket path where nothing listens is how to stay on inotify on purpose.
`GET /watch` reports the active `backend` (`inotify`, `fanotify`, `fsevents`,
`readdirectorychangesw`) and, when a repository is not on the broker, `backend_reason`
— what the probe met; `mf watch status` prints it.

!! The budget regime's placement

One non-recursive watch per ''eligible'' directory, found by the walk reconcile uses:
through eligible directories only, never following a symlinked directory (so a watch
never escapes the root — a Wine `dosdevices/z: -> /` is not traversed), skipping an
unreadable directory rather than failing. A fresh repository (`mf_watch = false`
everywhere) is watched nowhere. The root is the walk's base, not a gate: a directory
with `mf_watch = true` is watched even when the root is not. The set follows directories
appearing and disappearing, and is recomputed after a write changing `mf_watch`,
`mf_ignore` or `mfr_watch_exceeded`. An offline mount point is pruned like an
ineligible directory until it is back (spec-file-tracking "Offline subtrees").

!! What does not vary

Both regimes share the eligibility filter, the hard skip of `.metafolder/internal/`
(matched by absolute path, since `.metafolder/` may live anywhere — the daemon's own
writes must not feed back into the stream; the rest of `.metafolder/` is ordinary
content), offline subtrees, batching and event semantics. The vocabulary too:
`mf_watch`/`mf_ignore` say what should be covered, `mfr_watch_exceeded` says "leave this
subtree uncovered" in both. Only the budget's own answers are budget-only:
`watch_budget` and `watched_dirs` (both `null` under coverage), and the `unwatched`
reason of a watch check.

!! When the broker goes away

The coverage source's broker is a separate process. A disconnect, a reconnect and an
overflow are each announced by a diagnostic naming `mf reconcile`, and the gap closes
with that reconcile — the posture of a daemon that was down. Reconciling stays manual:
not after an overflow (it comes from load, a reconcile would add to it), not after a
reconnection (a flaky socket would fire one per blink).

!! Renames

`notify` correlates the two halves of a rename inside the watched tree into one
`Rename(Both)`; a lone `Rename(From)` means the file left, a lone `Rename(To)` that it
came from outside. Where a backend cannot correlate, a rename surfaces as `Remove` +
`Create` and identity is ''not'' recovered inline: the old metarecord is orphaned until
`mf orphan relink`. Under load `notify` itself may split a rename; the inotify cookie
travels with each half, and the flush fuses them back ([[Event batching]]). fanotify's
`FAN_RENAME` carries both sides in one event, and sees a move in or out of the repository
as a move.
